  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 100 AM Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... As the remnants of Isaias track through the \nTennessee Valley and push tropical moisture northeastward, Central \nNC will experience unsettling weather with showers and storms from \nSaturday afternoon/evening through Sunday night.\n\nRain chances increase Saturday afternoon, with widespread rainfall \ndriven by strong isentropic lift peaking between Saturday evening \nand Sunday. Models show a brief lull midday Sunday before a final \nbroken band of showers and storms passes through Sunday afternoon \nand evening. Conditions will clear rapidly from west to east \novernight into Monday morning.\n\nStorm totals across Central NC will average 1 to 2 inches, though \nlocalized urban or poor-drainage flooding is possible. Cold Air \nDamming set up will keep totals lower across the NW Piedmont (~0.5 \nto 1 inch), while a secondary low development along the coast will \nresult in localized higher amounts across the eastern Piedmont and \nCoastal Plain.\n\nThe severe threat remains low overall due to limited instability in \na high-shear, Low-CAPE environment. However, a few isolated strong \nto severe storms capable of damaging wind gusts cannot be ruled out \nSunday afternoon/evening, primarily across the far eastern and \nsoutheastern areas.\n\nThe CAD wedge will keep conditions cool and damp over the weekend, \nwith highs in the mid/upper 60s NW to low 70s in the SE and breezy \neasterly flow before sunshine returns Monday afternoon.",
